{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.21370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.21370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.21370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yash","familyName":"Turkar","name":"Turkar, Yash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yashom","familyName":"Dighe","name":"Dighe, Yashom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Dantu","name":"Dantu, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language-in-the-Loop Culvert Inspection on the Erie Canal"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:28:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:01:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-13T05:03:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:21:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Culverts on canals such as the Erie Canal, built originally in 1825, require frequent inspections to ensure safe operation. Human inspection of culverts is challenging due to age, geometry, poor illumination, weather, and lack of easy access. We introduce VISION, an end-to-end, language-in-the-loop autonomy system that couples a web-scale vision-language model (VLM) with constrained viewpoint planning for autonomous inspection of culverts. Brief prompts to the VLM solicit open-vocabulary ROI proposals with rationales and confidences, stereo depth is fused to recover scale, and a planner -- aware of culvert constraints -- commands repositioning moves to capture targeted close-ups. Deployed on a quadruped in a culvert under the Erie Canal, VISION closes the see, decide, move, re-image loop on-board and produces high-resolution images for detailed reporting without domain-specific fine-tuning. In an external evaluation by New York Canal Corporation personnel, initial ROI proposals achieved 61.4\\% agreement with subject-matter experts, and final post-re-imaging assessments reached 80\\%, indicating that VISION converts tentative hypotheses into grounded, expert-aligned findings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"First two authors contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.21370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-29T02:05:47Z","registered":"2025-09-29T02:05:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-16T03:00:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19391","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19391","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.19391","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel","familyName":"Marmoret","name":"Marmoret, Axel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reda","familyName":"Bensaid","name":"Bensaid, Reda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Lys","name":"Lys, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Gripon","name":"Gripon, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"François","familyName":"Leduc-Primeau","name":"Leduc-Primeau, François","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TensLoRA: Tensor Alternatives for Low-Rank Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7; I.2.10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:15:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:02:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T17:51:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:43:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T08:34:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:33:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) is widely used to efficiently adapt Transformers by adding trainable low-rank matrices to attention projections. While effective, these matrices are considered independent for each attention projection (Query, Key, and Value) and each layer. Recent extensions have considered joint, tensor-based adaptations, but only in limited forms and without a systematic framework. We introduce TensLoRA, a unified framework that aggregates LoRA updates into higher-order tensors and models a broad family of tensor-based low-rank adaptations. Our formulation generalizes existing tensor-based methods and enables mode-specific compression rates, allowing parameter budgets to be tailored according to the modality and task. Experiments on vision and language benchmarks reveal that the tensor construction directly impacts performance, sometimes better than standard LoRA under similar parameter counts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at ICASSP 2026. 5 pages, 1 figure, 2 tables. Code can be found at https://github.com/ax-le/TensLoRA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19391","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-25T01:43:55Z","registered":"2025-09-25T01:43:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:21:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18234","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18234","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18234","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeya Maria Jose","familyName":"Valanarasu","name":"Valanarasu, Jeya Maria Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noel CF","familyName":"Codella","name":"Codella, Noel CF","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reuben","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Reuben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianchu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qianchu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghui","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Guanghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoto","familyName":"Usuyama","name":"Usuyama, Naoto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cliff","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Cliff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"HoHin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, HoHin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Praneeth","familyName":"Sanapathi","name":"Sanapathi, Praneeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Hilado","name":"Hilado, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tristan","familyName":"Naumann","name":"Naumann, Tristan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier","familyName":"Alvarez-Valle","name":"Alvarez-Valle, Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khalil","familyName":"Malik","name":"Malik, Khalil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lidong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Lidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Horvitz","name":"Horvitz, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew P.","familyName":"Lungren","name":"Lungren, Matthew P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doug","familyName":"Burger","name":"Burger, Doug","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Topol","name":"Topol, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoifung","familyName":"Poon","name":"Poon, Hoifung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Vozila","name":"Vozila, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Illusion of Readiness in Health AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:48:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:03:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-01T17:21:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:09:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-11T20:55:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:07:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have demonstrated remarkable performance in a wide range of medical benchmarks. Yet underneath the seemingly promising results lie salient growth areas, especially in cutting-edge frontiers such as multimodal reasoning. In this paper, we introduce a series of adversarial stress tests to systematically assess the robustness of flagship models and medical benchmarks. Our study reveals prevalent brittleness in the presence of simple adversarial transformations: leading systems can guess the right answer even with key inputs removed, yet may get confused by the slightest prompt alterations, while fabricating convincing yet flawed reasoning traces. Using clinician-guided rubrics, we demonstrate that popular medical benchmarks vary widely in what they truly measure. Our study reveals significant competency gaps of frontier AI in attaining real-world readiness for health applications. If we want AI to earn trust in healthcare, we must demand more than leaderboard wins and must hold AI systems accountable to ensure robustness, sound reasoning, and alignment with real medical demands."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18234","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:53:28Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:53:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-15T02:57:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17970","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17970","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17970","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunchu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yunchu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojun","familyName":"Nan","name":"Nan, Zhaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhisheng","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Zhisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Joint Memory Frequency and Computing Frequency Scaling for Energy-efficient DNN Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:20:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:45:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:59:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:25:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-28T11:28:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:06:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep neural networks (DNNs) have been widely applied in diverse applications, but the problems of high latency and energy overhead are inevitable on resource-constrained devices. To address this challenge, most researchers focus on the dynamic voltage and frequency scaling (DVFS) technique to balance the latency and energy consumption by changing the computing frequency of processors. However, the adjustment of memory frequency is usually ignored and not fully utilized to achieve efficient DNN inference, which also plays a significant role in the inference time and energy consumption. In this paper, we first investigate the impact of joint memory frequency and computing frequency scaling on the inference time and energy consumption with a model-based and data-driven method. Then by combining with the fitting parameters of different DNN models, we give a preliminary analysis for the proposed model to see the effects of adjusting memory frequency and computing frequency simultaneously. Finally, simulation results in local inference and cooperative inference cases further validate the effectiveness of jointly scaling the memory frequency and computing frequency to reduce the energy consumption of devices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17970","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:24:33Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:24:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:15:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.22703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.22703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.22703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srikant","familyName":"Panda","name":"Panda, Srikant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hitesh Laxmichand","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Hitesh Laxmichand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AccessEval: Benchmarking Disability Bias in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:49:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:01:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed across diverse domains but often exhibit disparities in how they handle real-life queries. To systematically investigate these effects within various disability contexts, we introduce \\textbf{AccessEval (Accessibility Evaluation)}, a benchmark evaluating 21 closed- and open-source LLMs across 6 real-world domains and 9 disability types using paired Neutral and Disability-Aware Queries. We evaluated model outputs with metrics for sentiment, social perception, and factual accuracy.\n Our analysis reveals that responses to disability-aware queries tend to have a more negative tone, increased stereotyping, and higher factual error compared to neutral queries. These effects show notable variation by domain and disability type, with disabilities affecting hearing, speech, and mobility disproportionately impacted. These disparities reflect persistent forms of ableism embedded in model behavior.\n By examining model performance in real-world decision-making contexts, we better illuminate how such biases can translate into tangible harms for disabled users. This framing helps bridges the gap between technical evaluation and user impact, reinforcing importance of bias mitigation in day-to-day applications. Our dataset is publicly available at: https://huggingface.co/datasets/Srikant86/AccessEval"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-30T03:29:38Z","registered":"2025-09-30T03:29:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T03:29:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18043","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18043","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18043","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinlong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yinlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andre","familyName":"Keyser","name":"Keyser, Andre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dylan P.","familyName":"Losey","name":"Losey, Dylan P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prepare Before You Act: Learning From Humans to Rearrange Initial States"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:18:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:48:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Imitation learning (IL) has proven effective across a wide range of manipulation tasks. However, IL policies often struggle when faced with out-of-distribution observations; for instance, when the target object is in a previously unseen position or occluded by other objects. In these cases, extensive demonstrations are needed for current IL methods to reach robust and generalizable behaviors. But when humans are faced with these sorts of atypical initial states, we often rearrange the environment for more favorable task execution. For example, a person might rotate a coffee cup so that it is easier to grasp the handle, or push a box out of the way so they can directly grasp their target object. In this work we seek to equip robot learners with the same capability: enabling robots to prepare the environment before executing their given policy. We propose ReSET, an algorithm that takes initial states -- which are outside the policy's distribution -- and autonomously modifies object poses so that the restructured scene is similar to training data. Theoretically, we show that this two step process (rearranging the environment before rolling out the given policy) reduces the generalization gap. Practically, our ReSET algorithm combines action-agnostic human videos with task-agnostic teleoperation data to i) decide when to modify the scene, ii) predict what simplifying actions a human would take, and iii) map those predictions into robot action primitives. Comparisons with diffusion policies, VLAs, and other baselines show that using ReSET to prepare the environment enables more robust task execution with equal amounts of total training data. See videos at our project website: https://reset2025paper.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18043","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:26:16Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:26:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:26:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":6,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=%28doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.17970+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18043+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18234+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.19391+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.21370+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.22703%29"}}